# OSI Layer 1 – Physical Layer

## What is the Physical Layer?
The Physical Layer is responsible for the **actual transmission of raw bits (0s and 1s)** over a physical medium.

It defines **how data is sent**, not **what the data means**.

---

## Why the Physical Layer is Needed?
- To physically connect devices
- To define signal transmission rules
- To ensure bits are transferred correctly

Without this layer, no data communication is possible.

---

## Key Responsibilities
- Transmission of binary data
- Defines voltage levels
- Defines cable types and connectors
- Bit synchronization
- Data rate control

---

## What the Physical Layer Includes
- Cables (Ethernet, Fiber, Coaxial)
- Network Interface Cards (NIC)
- Voltage levels
- Hubs
- Repeaters

---

## Data Representation
- Data is transmitted as:
  - Electrical signals (copper cables)
  - Light signals (fiber optics)
  - Radio waves (wireless)

---

## Devices Working at Physical Layer
| Device | Purpose |
|-----|-------|
| Hub | Broadcasts signals |
| Repeater | Regenerates signal |
| NIC | Converts data to signals |
| Cables | Carry signals |

---

## Real-World Example
If an Ethernet cable is unplugged:
- Physical layer fails
- No data transmission happens
- Higher layers are irrelevant

---

## Common Issues & Troubleshooting
- Cable unplugged or damaged
- Incorrect cable type
- Network interface disabled
- Hardware failure

